description:
Manage software breakpoints over a block of code the way a debugger does (lesson 3.01). The code is a byte buffer; address `base + i` is byte `i`.

```cpp
class Breakpoints
{
public:
    Breakpoints(std::vector<std::uint8_t>& code, std::uint64_t base);
    bool set(std::uint64_t address);
    bool remove(std::uint64_t address);
    bool isSet(std::uint64_t address) const;
    std::vector<std::uint8_t> view() const;
    std::optional<std::uint64_t> onBreakpoint(std::uint64_t& rip);
    void onSingleStep();
};
```

- **set** saves the original byte and writes `0xCC`. Setting one that is already set, or an address outside the code, returns false.
- **remove** writes the original byte back. False if there is no breakpoint there.
- **view** returns the code as the user should see it: with the original bytes in place of every `CC` you wrote.
- **onBreakpoint** is called when the target stops on an `int3` with `rip` already past it. If `rip - 1` is one of your breakpoints: move `rip` back by one, put the original byte back so the instruction can run, remember that this breakpoint must be **re-armed**, and return its address. Otherwise (an `int3` that was in the program itself) return `std::nullopt` and change nothing.
- **onSingleStep** is called after the target has executed that one instruction: write `0xCC` again for the breakpoint waiting to be re-armed, if it is still set.

